From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] crash_dump: a limit this CPU can meet

A dump of system_server takes 4 s on this 996 MHz single core (measured on PW3
Android Test V49, where native dumps started working). AOSP's 5 s limit, meant
for a dump that hangs, would cut short exactly the dumps with the most threads
and the most to say. The crashing process is stopped while the dump runs and
nothing else waits on it, so the limit is set from the slowest dump measured,
with room: 20 s.

--- a/debuggerd/crash_dump.cpp
+++ b/debuggerd/crash_dump.cpp
@@ -356,7 +356,13 @@
   //
   // Note: processes with many threads and minidebug-info can take a bit to
   //       unwind, do not make this too small. b/62828735
-  alarm(5);
+  //
+  // PW3: a dump of system_server takes 4 s on this 996 MHz single core (Test
+  // V49), so 5 s would truncate the dumps that matter most. The limit is only
+  // there to free a process whose dump hangs, and the crashing process is
+  // stopped while it runs, so it is set from the slowest dump measured, with
+  // room: 20 s.
+  alarm(20);
 
   std::string attach_error;
 
